WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
A local move costs several hundred dollars while the truck burns 48 cents a mile. This is

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.380
First Principles Daily, episode 128. Picture a modest house across town. The quote arrives

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.860
as an hourly rate and the total climbs into the hundreds before anyone has argued about

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:21.900
a piano. The truck is not the expensive part of that afternoon. Diesel sits near 48 cents

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.620
a mile. Equipment ownership adds about 40 cents more. The spread is the opening. Prices

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:32.180
This is built around people standing in your driveway, not around fuel burned or tires

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.540
and brakes wearing down one trip at a time.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.280
Local residential moves are sold by crew time, not by distance or weight.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.500
Service anchor reports typical 2026 rates of 90 to $150 an hour for a two-person crew with

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.540
a truck, and $130 to $200 for three people with two hour or three hour minimums.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.800
The clock starts when the crew arrives.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.720
It stops only after the last item is set down inside the new place.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:04.100
An extra flight of stairs, a narrow hallway, or a request to pack extends that clock and

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:04.880
raises the bill.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.180
Carriers also carry liability insurance, workers' compensation coverage, and seasonal capacity

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.560
that sits idle for months.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:18.100
Dispatch and sales overhead, vehicle licensing, and the cost of turning work away in peak

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.800
summer weeks all fold into the same hourly quote.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.420
most people move once or twice in a lifetime, so comparison shopping feels

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:30.240
expensive in time, and the structure sticks. The final bill therefore tracks

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.000
coordination layers and risk allocation more than the cost of rolling the

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.500
truck another mile. Spend an extra 40 minutes wrestling a sofa around a tight

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:43.460
corner, and you pay those 40 minutes at the full crew rate. The engine is idling,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:49.040
depreciation keeps its same steady pace. The meter does not care. A minimum hour

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.160
rule protects the carrier if the job finishes faster than expected. It also blocks you from

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.820
keeping the savings when the physical work turns out lighter than average. Seasonal demand

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:04.560
stacks on top. In many cities, the number of available crews have is between January and July,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.020
so summer quotes embed the cost of trucks parked through the winter. Each job is still treated

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:15.720
as a one-off. The carrier cannot spread a dispatch office or annual permits across a steady stream

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.880
of moves. The hourly model survives because it dumps every layer of uncertainty and idle

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.740
time onto whoever needs the truck that day, not because it matches the physics of moving

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.400
mass from one address to another.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.440
Now put a number on the floor. The American Transportation Research Institute's 2026 update

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:40.480
places the 2025 industry average marginal operating cost for four higher trucks at $2.34

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:47.100
a mile. Fuel is 48.2 cents of that. Truck plus trailer ownership or lease payments are

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:53.560
another 40.4 cents. The remaining dollar in 45 cents covers maintenance, tires, insurance,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:59.820
tolls, and driver wages when those items are treated as variable. A rough magic wand estimate

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:05.320
for one household starts with the two dominant vehicle lines, energy in the diesel and capital

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:11.320
recovery on the truck itself. Take a typical three-hour local job covering perhaps 25 miles

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:18.460
round trip. Fuel sits near $12. Ownership sits near $10. Add a modest allowance for tires

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.820
and unscheduled repairs and the physics-based floor for the vehicle portion alone still stays

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:30.940
below $40. That is the Magic Wand number. Arrange the atoms of fuel, steel, and rubber into a

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:36.400
trip, and you are still under $40 for the machine. The build price for that same job

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:43.040
commonly lands between $400 and $900 once crew time, minimums, and overhead are included.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:50.600
Divide the low end by the floor. 400 divided by 40 is 10. Divide the high end. 900 divided

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:56.840
by 40 is a bit over 20, so the idiot index lands roughly between 10 and 20. The finished

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.180
Afternoon costs something like 10 to 20 times the vehicle physics underneath it.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.040
That is an estimate you can check, not a measured laboratory result.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.560
Change the mileage or the build total and the ratio moves.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.140
The direction does not.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:14.060
The gap is not in the steel of the truck bed or the energy in the fuel.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.140
It lives in scheduling friction, insurance stacked on top of insurance.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:23.440
The seasonal mismatch between demand and fleet size, and the hand-assembly nature of each

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:23.820
job.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:29.380
Because every move is still a bespoke event, sales, temporary parking permits, and the

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:34.600
inventory of unused trucks and slow months get amortized across very few billable hours.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.840
The index points at coordination and utilization, not at new materials, run the same truck

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:46.420
300 miles in a day instead of 25, and the per mile ownership cost drops by more than

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.420
half, even before any change in fuel efficiency.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:53.900
The current price gives the carrier no direct incentive to chase that utilization.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.260
Insurance shows the same arithmetic.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.380
A policy written for a year spreads its premium across every mile the truck actually rolls.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.240
When utilization falls, the premium per mile rises sharply, and that increase passes straight

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.320
into the hourly rate.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.600
A redesign starts by attacking the minimum hour rule.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:16.600
Shift more moves, even inside one metro area, to a weight and distance quote.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.040
That needs accurate portable scales, already common on long haul trailers.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:30.340
Next, standardized truck configurations and loading patterns, so the same vehicle can turn in four hours instead of sitting through multi-hour gaps between jobs.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:39.620
Third, move routine insurance and liability into a shared pool or a usage based product that charges per mile rather than per annual policy.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.160
That trims the fixed cost layer now inflating every quote.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:49.940
Each step needs reliable real-time demand data, standardized cargo manifests, and either regulatory

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:55.020
acceptance of digital bills of lading, or a carrier willing to self-insure a larger slice

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:55.520
of risk.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.100
The hardest constraint is seasonal peaking.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.000
Summer demand can exceed winter supply by factors of two or three in many cities.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:08.320
An operator that smooths that curve with off-peak pricing or shared municipal staging yards

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.200
captures most of what is left.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.140
None of this needs new physics.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:17.240
It needs consistent measurement of the per-mile floor already published by the American Transportation

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.100
Research Institute, and a willingness to price against that floor instead of last year's

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:22.900
hourly precedent.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:28.000
A flat per-mile rate for local work still has to match trucks to jobs in real-time.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:32.440
Without that matching layer, the trucks simply spend more time empty between loads.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:37.800
Pulling insurance across small operators needs verifiable mileage records and loss data.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:43.040
India. Most local moving companies do not collect those records in usable form. Once those

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:48.580
data and coordination problems are solved, the prize is visible. The same 25-mile job

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:55.700
whose vehicle floor sits near $40 could be priced closer to $120-$150. Once crew time

00:06:55.700 --> 00:07:00.620
is paid only for actual loading and unloading, rather than for the whole elapsed appointment.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:05.940
That remaining band is still well above the Magic Wand number. People still have to

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:11.140
lift sofas. The point is that most of the idiot index is coordination, not muscle or

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:16.620
diesel. One principle sits under the whole case. An hourly meter running on human presence

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.760
will always diverge from a cost meter running on fuel and steel, unless someone deliberately

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:27.260
reconciles the two. Leave them separate, and the customer pays for waiting. For winter trucks

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:31.460
and for the chance that the sofas sticks in the stairwell, all at the same rate as

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:36.980
useful work. A second principle, any service whose price is set by calendar friction rather

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:42.240
than physical throughput carries an embedded seasonal tax. Only shared capacity or demand

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.820
shifting removes it. You cannot wish July into January. You can price the empty months so

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.960
some move slide, or you can let several carriers share a yard and a truck instead of each holding

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:58.280
a private buffer. The same split shows up wherever a person hour stands in for a physical unit.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:03.400
unit. The unit might be a mile, a kilogram, a kilowatt hour. If the invoice ignores

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:08.580
the unit, the idiot index hides inside the schedule. Household goods are a clean place

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:14.280
to see it because the floor is already published, mile by mile for four higher trucks. The habit

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:19.220
in front of that floor is an appointment clock. Tomorrow the series continues with one further

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:24.560
concrete case or one further industry whose raw material floor is still hidden by accumulated

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.880
The first visible signal that someone is attempting this in household goods will be a published

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:35.480
per mile quote that stays flat when crew size changes and rises only with actual distance

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:35.980
and weight.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.860
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.820
The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.640
Send this to a fellow builder.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:45.320
I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:46.600
